.TH TIMBER\-OTLP 1 "2026-08-15" "timberfs" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
timber\-otlp \- ship a timberfs entry stream to an OTLP/HTTP receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timber\-otlp
.RB [ \-\-endpoint
.IR URL ]
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ STORE ]
.br
.B timberfs query \-\-records
.I ...
.B | timber\-otlp
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B timber\-otlp
posts a store's log ENTRIES \(em a stamped line plus its continuation
lines, so a stack trace stays one record \(em to an OTLP/HTTP receiver:
an OpenTelemetry Collector, or any backend that speaks OTLP. One
.B LogRecord
per entry.
.PP
It is a
.BR reader .
It consumes the
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
stream that
.B timberfs query \-\-records
produces (spawning it for a
.I STORE
positional, exactly as
.BR timber\-filter (1)
does), so the append path is untouched and an unreachable receiver can
stall the shipper and nothing else. The
.B store is the send buffer:
where a collector's queue is sized by guessing, retention is the
disconnection budget \(em
.B retain 30d
means the receiver can be gone for thirty days \(em and any window can
be re-shipped afterwards, which a collector cannot do because it
retains nothing.
.PP
Raw text is refused rather than guessed at: only the record stream
carries the write times and logline stamps this needs.
.SH MODES
Two, because they need different time axes.
.TP
.B Follow
.B \-\-follow
with
.B \-\-cursor
is the durable shipper: entries are posted as they are committed, and
the position is persisted on the WRITE axis \(em the only monotonic one,
since a logline stamp can move backwards and resuming from one would
skip a late-arriving entry permanently.
.TP
.B Replay
.BR \-\-from / \-\-to
selects a LOGLINE window \(em \(lqre-ship what happened during the
incident\(rq \(em and takes no cursor: a replay is a deliberate act, not
a resumable one.
.SH THE CURSOR
A cursor is CONSUMER state, so it lives at a path you name, outside the
store: several consumers read one store at different positions, and a
cursor must not travel inside a
.B .timber
bundle as if it were provenance. It is a flat JSON object; rewinding by
editing
.B seq
is the supported edit.
.PP
The position is
.RI ( seq ", " n ):
the NUMBER of the chunk an entry arrived in, and how many entries of that
chunk were already delivered. A chunk number is the only axis that cannot
move backwards \(em the write axis is a wall clock an NTP step can push
back, and an intake stamps the sender's event time on purpose (see THE TWO
CLOCKS in
.BR timberfs (1)),
and a cursor is a single point, where a range or a prefix would have
survived. Positioning is chunk-granular, so a resume re-reads the boundary
chunk and drops the prefix it already sent. Delivery is therefore
.B at-least-once
\(em which is all OTLP offers anyway, having no deduplication key. The
cursor is written after the receiver accepts, never before, so a crash
re-delivers instead of skipping.
.B wl
is also recorded, but only to orient a human reading the file; it decides
nothing.
.PP
An entry read from the LIVE EDGE (a store with
.B wal
declared, tailed within the second) carries no chunk number, its chunk not
existing yet. It is delivered and counted, but the position does not move:
there is nowhere inside an unwritten chunk to resume from. A restart
therefore re-reads from the last chunk boundary \(em bounded, and the same
thing chunk-granular resume already does.
.PP
A cursor written before chunk numbers existed holds a write time instead.
It is converted on first use: the time resolves to a chunk the way
.B \-\-from
does, that chunk is re-read from its start (so up to one chunk is
re-delivered, and nothing is skipped), and the converted file is written by
the first save AFTER a successful send \(em never ahead of a durability
proof.
.PP
The store is identified by its
.B .bark
id when it declares one (identity, not address: a moved store still
matches), else by its canonical path. Declare an identity with
.B timberfs set
to make a cursor survive a move.
.PP
On every resume the backlog still ahead of the cursor is reported. The
exception is a
.BR GAP :
retention acts on the head of a store and nothing coordinates it with
this shipper's progress, so a shipper down longer than the store's
.B retain
window comes back to find the chunk its cursor points at already
dropped. That is a WARNING naming exactly how many chunks were dropped
unread \(em a count, the numbers stating it rather than a timestamp
comparison implying it \(em and then
the run continues from the oldest chunk \(em the loss is already in the
past, and a shipper that refuses to start ships nothing. Register this
shipper as a FOLLOWER of the store
.RB ( "timberfs follower create" )
and the same lag is visible from the store's side, in
.B timberfs list
and
.BR "timberfs info" ,
before it becomes loss \(em see
.B FOLLOWERS
in
.BR timberfs (1).
A registered follower is also run BY name:
.B timberfs follower run
supplies
.BR \-\-follow ,
.B \-\-cursor
and the endpoint from the declaration, so a
.B timberfs\-follower@
unit needs no flags of its own.
.SH MAPPING
.TP
.B time
.B timeUnixNano
is the entry's own logline stamp, falling back to arrival when the line
carries none;
.B observedTimeUnixNano
is always the write time of the chunk it arrived in. OTLP's two time
fields are timberfs's two axes.
.TP
.B body
The entry's bytes verbatim, minus the trailing newline. Multiline
entries stay one body.
.TP
.B severity
The first UPPERCASE level word in the entry's FIRST line
(TRACE/DEBUG/INFO/NOTICE/WARN/ERROR/FATAL/CRITICAL and friends), mapped
to the OTLP severity numbers. Case-sensitive on purpose: \(lqconnection
error\(rq is not an ERROR. Other conventions need
.BR \-\-severity\-regex .
.TP
.B resource
What the STORE is:
.B service.name
from
.BR \-\-service ,
else the
.B .bark
\(lqservice\(rq, else the store's name; plus
.BR host.name ,
.B timberfs.store.id
and
.BR timberfs.store.path .
.B \-\-resource
overrides any of them.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Destination
.TP
.BI \-\-endpoint " URL"
A base URL (\fB/v1/logs\fR is appended) or the signal URL itself.
Default:
.BR $OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_LOGS_ENDPOINT ,
.BR $OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT ,
else
.BR http://localhost:4318 .
Plaintext
.B http
only: terminate TLS in a collector or proxy beside the shipper.
.TP
.BI \-\-header " K=V"
Extra request header (repeatable).
.B $OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS
is also read.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " DUR"
Connect/read/write timeout per request (default 10s).
.SS What is sent
.TP
.BI \-\-service " NAME"
Resource
.BR service.name .
.TP
.BI \-\-resource " K=V"
Extra resource attribute (repeatable); overrides derived ones.
.TP
.BI \-\-severity\-regex " PATTERN"
Where the level is, when it is not an uppercase level word. The first
capture group (else the whole match) becomes
.BR severityText ;
a name that maps to no OTLP level keeps the text and claims no number.
.TP
.BI \-\-batch\-size " N"
Maximum LogRecords per export request (default 512).
.TP
.BI \-\-batch\-timeout " DUR"
Send a partial batch after this long with nothing new (default 1s).
.TP
.BI \-\-encoding " proto|json"
Wire encoding (default
.BR proto ,
what every OTLP sender defaults to and what a receiver is likeliest to
accept).
.B json
is readable on the wire; both carry the same request.
.TP
.BI \-\-compress " none|gzip"
Compress request bodies (default
.BR none ).
Worth it over a network, noise over loopback.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Print the export requests instead of sending them, always in the JSON
spelling \(em the two encodings carry the same request, and one of them is
readable. A cursor is read but never advanced.
.SS Position
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
Keep shipping as entries are committed.
.TP
.BI \-\-cursor " PATH"
Persist the position here (requires
.BR \-\-follow ).
.TP
.BI \-\-start " end|begin"
Where to start when the cursor file does not exist yet (default
.BR end ).
.TP
.BI \-\-from " TIME" ", " \-\-to " TIME"
Replay window, in the formats
.BR timberfs (1)
takes (TIME FORMATS).
.SS Other
.TP
.B \-\-quiet
Suppress progress notes on standard error; errors still print.
.SH DELIVERY
A retryable answer (429, 502, 503, 504, or a transport failure) is
retried with capped exponential backoff, honouring
.BR Retry\-After ,
forever: the store holds the backlog, and giving up would turn a
reachable-again receiver into lost data. Any other 4xx/5xx is permanent
per the OTLP specification \(em the batch is dropped, loudly, because a
stream that can never advance is worse.
.B partialSuccess
inside a 200 is counted and reported.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the stream ended cleanly (a bounded replay), non-zero on a
configuration error or a truncated record stream. A followed stream is
unbounded and ends at interrupt.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Ship a store as it is written, resumably:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timber\-otlp \-\-follow \-\-cursor /var/lib/timberfs/app.otlp \\
    \-\-endpoint http://collector:4318 backing/app.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
Replay an incident window into a fresh backend:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timber\-otlp \-\-from '2026\-08\-11 14:00' \-\-to '2026\-08\-11 15:00' \\
    \-\-endpoint http://new\-backend:4318 backing/app.log
.EE
.RE
.PP
See what would be sent, without a receiver:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timber\-otlp \-\-dry\-run backing/app.log | jq .
.EE
.RE
.PP
Ship only what matters, by filtering first:
.PP
.RS
.EX
timber\-filter \-\-has ERROR \-\-records backing/app.log | timber\-otlp
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR timberfs (1),
.BR timber\-filter (1),
.BR timberfs\-records (5)
